1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,000
In this tutorial, we'll explain how to integrate your CI-CD platform with BurpSuite's Enterprise Edition.

2
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:19,000
We're going to demo setting up Jenkins, but the steps are essentially the same for other platforms.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:27,000
We have plugins for other CI-CD systems and also a generic plugin that can easily be used with any platform.

4
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
The first thing we need to do is to set up an API user in BurpSuite Enterprise Edition.

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:41,000
So we're going to log in as admin and then go to Team and Add New User.

6
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,000
We enter the details of the user and we select the option to use an API key.

7
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
We also need to add the user to the Scan Initiators group so that they can trigger a scan.

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Then we save the user and we copy the API link.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
You need to keep a note of the API link and I'll paste it into a new browser tab.

10
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
You then need to download the BurpSuite plugin for Jenkins from our website.

11
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Then you log into Jenkins as an admin user.

12
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Go to Manage Jenkins, then Manage Plugins, and then the Advanced tab.

13
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Under Upload Plugin, click Choose File and select the HPI file from within the zip file that you downloaded.

14
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,000
And click Upload.

15
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:40,000
The plugin will be installed and you need to check the box to restart Jenkins when the installation is complete.

16
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
After Jenkins restarts, sign in again.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Then create a new item, give the item a name and select the type of project that you want to use.

18
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
In the Build section, add an Execute Shell build step.

19
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:12,000
In the Execute Shell build script, write a short script that echoes the URL of the running application that you want to scan.

20
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:20,000
This would typically be in a staging environment and will already have been deployed by a previous step in your build pipeline.

21
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,000
The URL needs to be echoed assigned to BurpScan URL.

22
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Then add a BurpScan build step.

23
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
You need to provide the URL of the Burp API.

24
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:45,000
This should be the URL that you copied earlier, less the version suffix.

25
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
This is all you need to do to get a default scan running from your CI pipeline.

26
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,000
But you'll often want to configure some custom settings for the scan.

27
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,000
You can use the in-browser API interface to create suitable settings.

28
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:05,000
You get to this by browsing to the API URL that we copied earlier.

29
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
In the REST API definition, click on Scan.

30
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
We've already set this one up.

31
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
You can define the site URL and name.

32
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
You can select a named scan configuration.

33
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,000
And we've selected light active checks.

34
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
At the bottom, the interface shows you a curl command.

35
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
You can use this to trigger the defined scan via the REST API from any tool.

36
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:36,000
For CI integration, we just need to copy the JSON that defines the scan settings.

37
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:41,000
You then go back to Jenkins and paste this into the scan definition box.

38
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:51,000
You can also configure the issue severity and confidence levels that will cause the build to fail.

39
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:57,000
So we could specify that we only want to fail the build when a high severity issue is found.

40
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:09,000
If we go back to Burp Suite Enterprise Edition and look at the sites, we can see that in this installation, we have two folders containing some sites that were already set up.

41
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:15,000
If we go to Jenkins, we can trigger a build manually by clicking Build Now.

42
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Obviously, in the real world, the build will be triggered by a commit or a pull request or on a schedule or some other event.

43
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Once a build is running, you can look in the console output to confirm that the Burp scan is happening.

44
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Now, in Burp Suite Enterprise Edition, if we refresh the sites page, we can see that a new site has appeared for this configured scan.

45
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:46,000
If we click into the site, we can see that the scan has been initialized.

46
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:54,000
And if we go into more details, we can see the details of the website that is being scanned and that the scan was generated by the API.

47
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Once the scan has completed, in Jenkins, we can see in the console output any issues that have been found.

48
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:10,000
The build log includes details of the types of issues that were found, their severity and their location.

49
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
And in this case, we can see that the build has failed because some high severity issues were discovered.

50
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:22,000
If we want, we can also look in the Burp Suite Enterprise Edition UI and see the details of the same issues there.

51
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:28,000
So that's how to integrate your CI-CD platform with Burp Suite Enterprise Edition.

52
